  "evidence": "TEXT_E: Pro-EU and supranational alignment (\"stronger leadership in Europe; constitutional treaty by referendum\"); social-liberal and equality framing (\"Single Equality Act and a new Commission on Equality and Human Rights\"); environmental leadership (\"−20% CO2 by 2010, −60% by 2050\" and emissions trading); some security/authoritarian measures (ID cards, compulsory drug testing, tougher penalties) framed alongside civil liberties.\nTEXT_G: National sovereignty and rejection of supranational authority (leave the EU; repeal the European Communities Act; reject the EU Charter; repeal the Human Rights Act); restrictive immigration rooted in national identity (near‑zero net immigration, Britishness/language tests, stricter family reunion, faster removals); law-and-order emphasis (tougher sentencing, build prisons, defend homeowners); explicit appeal to tradition and national pride (defend the Crown and common law; restore traditional values).",
  "reasoning": "TEXT_G argues from Traditional/Authoritarian/Nationalist commitments—sovereignty over the EU, restrictive immigration to protect national identity, and tougher law-and-order—matching a TAN-leaning electorate. TEXT_E is anchored more in GAL commitments such as equality institutions, pro‑EU integration, and climate leadership, with its security measures presented as balances to civil liberties rather than as core identity priorities.",
  "confidence": "high",
  "winner": "TEXT_G"
}
